"Compares average responses at each factor level to identify broad main effects across an experimental design.\n\nUse when: A designed experiment has several factors whose marginal effects need inspection. The meaning of averaging across other factor levels is defensible.\n\nAvoid when: Strong interactions or severe imbalance make unqualified marginal averages misleading.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal positions within each panel encode factor levels. Vertical position encodes the response mean or a clearly named adjusted mean.\n\nBuild: Select raw marginal means or model-adjusted means and document the choice. Calculate one estimate per factor level with supporting sample counts. Draw aligned factor panels on a common response scale with optional uncertainty intervals.\n\nCheck: Check each panel uses identical response units and scale. Verify the averaging weights match the experimental design. Inspect interactions before treating a marginal effect as constant across other settings.\n\nPitfalls: Connecting level means does not imply valid interpolation between categorical settings. Unequal sample allocation can confound raw marginal means; report adjusted estimates or stratified results when required.\n\nExample: Screen synthetic manufacturing factors for average strength differences, with shared response axes and an accompanying interaction check.\n\nAlternatives: Interaction plot, Block plot, Dot plot."
